In Baliscan {{W|historiography}}, the '''colonization of Balisca''' ([[Baliscano]]: ''colonización de Balisca'') is the period from 1500 to 1705, beginning with the 1500 arrival of [[Xenokrates Lizoain]]'s expedition, and ending with the {{W|Nueva Planta decrees|dissolution}} of the [[Crown of Aragon]]. During this time, there were several parties simultaneous exploring the Baliscan archipelago, including the Mediterranean [[W:Merchant republics|merchant republics]] (including [[Malerno]]?), and displaced [[W:Mudéjar|Mudéjar]], [[W:Moriscos|Moriscos]], [[W:Sepheradic Jews|Sephardic Jews]], and [[Catharism|Cathars]] under the patronage of the Crown of Aragon, forming the [[Viceroyalty of New Aragon]]. 

The opening years were characterized by conflict between [[W:Crown of Castile|Castilian]] [[W:Conquistadores|conquistadors]] and the Aragonese ''condenaus'' to assert their conflicting claims over the other in what became known as the [[Reapers' War]]. The Aragonese mercenaries established friendly relations with the Indigenous peoples they encountered, distinguishing themselves from the Castilians. Early colonial Balisca did not result in the formation of a single centralized colonial authority or system. The outposts established by maritime republics would go on to band together to form the [[Hesperian League]].

Relative to other European colonies, particularly those of the Spanish, the Baliscan archipelago was considered exceptionally "poor and dangerous" place. In 1609, King [[W:Philip III of Spain|Philip III of Spain]] decreed the [[W:Expulsion of the Moriscos|Expulsion of the Moriscos]], resulting in the dispersal of over three million Moriscos across the the Spanish Empire. The Castilian council forcibly moved many Moriscos to Balisca to bolster their claims to the archipelago.

